at complicated  basically taylor's black ops group are  the good guys but they have to pull off  some bad things for the greater good big  picture stuff in between missions  there's a hub that i didn't really care  for you can show off your collectibles  read mission logs and wonder who the  hell these random people are it kind of  slowed down the pace for me because  they're unskippable you can also  customize your loadouts here which again  i didn't care for  call of duty's in the past have always  set you up with a pretty good default  loadout and left you room to explore  other weapons simply by picking them up  off the ground  but here you can't even do that what you  pick is what you're stuck with what the  hell oh my god  as you progress you start to hear  whispers of a frozen forest a seemingly  metaphoric place where everything is  okay in short it exists and it was  designed by a man named yusuf saleem but  taylor and his black ops team are  obsessed with finding this forest and  will do anything to get into it why  because the black ops team is infected  with a dni virus called corvus they  think the source of the corvus virus can  be found within this frozen forest and  if they can get there they'll be able to  purge it from all of their systems it's  a little confusing for a call of duty  game but it makes for some fun moments  in storytelling when you finally reach  the frozen force and purge the corvus  virus you come to and taylor's voice is  still in your head  so basically player and tailor  successfully purge the virus but  player's mind is sort of taken over by  taylor what's your name soldier  in a frozen forest i said what's your  name  taylor  so taylor might not be the best of guys  anyway i applaud this game's risks the  creative ending made it worth playing at  least once i still wouldn't call it a  very approachable call of duty but give  it a shot if you want something a little  off the beaten path  after merging with neversoft studios the  updated infinity war team envisioned an  all-new story set primarily in space and  since me and space still weren't getting  along i skipped call of duty this year  too but again i went into this with a  fresh perspective reminding myself that  i've been surprised by more than one  call of duty campaign before so i jumped  in and hoped that infinity ward would  not let me down  this game had some major advancements in  the series like seamless transitions  from ground to air nearly non-existent  loading times and a hub world straight  out of battlestar galactica it doesn't  feel like call of duty but i'm here for  it in the game's opening mission the  good guys are sent to secure some top  secret weapons when they're stopped by  the bad dudes and admiral koch the most  vapid villain in any call of duty game  which sucks because he's kit from game  of thrones isn't he i think he's just  written really poorly i hated seeing  this guy anyway after this first mission  goes sideways we meet lieutenant nick  reyes and nora salter in these opening  hours i was happy to witness some solid  writing except for koch  excellent motion capture and a really  polished presentation there's plenty of  military speak that goes in one ear and  out the other but the performances and  relationships kept me engaged from the  start  speaking of relationships after the  introduction we meet ethan he's a  lovable robot who's ready to serve his  military  it's hard for some people to trust him  at first being a robot and all but his  friendly nature and quick wit win over  even the toughest of soldiers  the navy's official policy for a  gunfight  in the marine  there are some fun standout missions  including dog fights in space and  fighting terrifying evil robots who have  been programmed to kill you all  accompanied by your buds ethan and  salter by the way reyes and salter come  on secret space romance  everyone else aboard the retribution  space station is important and memorable  at first they just feel like a crew but  as you see them with more frequency you  build relationships and trade stories  soon enough the crew feels more like a  family i think infinity ward felt this  way too because the ending credits paid  tribute to just about everyone you get  to hear them talk candidly about their  lives and experiences and sort of a dear  john style message they individually  record including ethan who sadly  sacrifices himself for reyes overall i  enjoyed the campaign way more than i  thought i would there were some pacing  issues and some strange character  development choices but the gameplay was  solid and the relationships you make  were simply delightful that's the last  time i let a call of duty set in space  stop me from giving it a shot  this was one of the biggest surprises of  the bunch 2017 was an important time for  me in call of duty history i started my  job at gamespot but i hadn't played a  call of duty game in years however when  i heard that this next title was boots  on the ground i told myself i was in no  matter what fortunately for me it was  world war ii developed by sledgehammer  games now i say fortunately because i  love this game but when i say that i get  some funny looks and i get it the series  was pushing further into the future that  meant exo suits cool tech flashy space  weapons and a blank canvas to create any  story they wanted a world war ii game  just felt like the opposite of that idea  taking several steps back in the eyes of  most fans under normal circumstances i  wouldn't want to play another world war  2 game either but the stars aligned for  me on this one and i was willing to give  anything that wasn't in space a shot  welcome to the bloody first  you're a long way from texas farm boy  world war ii was the refresh i needed i  spent hours in multiplayer so much so  that i never got around to the campaign  i was addicted to the prestige grind and  earning supply drops to get the newest  weapon skins i played war mode for hours  sweating it out to make the push deep  into enemy territory only to get pushed  back into my own i kept putting off the  campaign until now and i'm sad i did  because the story is just as good  right away you're treated to a quaint  and ominous opening aboard the ship with  your fellow soldiers i'm bad with names  so i feared i'd forget everyone's name  here entirely fortunately the game does  not allow this this is your crew the  soldiers you fight alongside with  throughout the entire campaign even josh  dumel who's a dick  as the division makes the push on to  normandy i felt like i was watching a  movie about it the pre-rendered  cutscenes are stunning not only that but  the dialogue and nervous banter are all  believably written in this vulnerable  opening i felt my stomach curl i felt  like it was taking forever to unload  onto the beach but when it happened i  still wasn't ready it was after this  emotional retelling of d-day that you  feel relieved to see some of your fellow  soldiers that made it it's here  afterwards where you can take a moment  to control and establish the systems and  mechanics that this game has there's no  regenerating health you can call on your  squad mates for scouting grenades health  packs and even artillery strikes on  veteran difficulty utilizing your  soldiers like this is a must it was  something new to me but useful in the  majority of the campaign  speaking of the campaign this is a  massive departure from the first three  call of duty games its presentation is  beautiful everything from the sound  design and writing to the score and set  pieces this game is a real treat to the  eyes and ears any drawbacks i had were  from the repetitive nature of some of  the tougher levels and again i think  that's only because i was playing on the  veteran difficulty but it's a call of  duty game after all and there are some  off-the-wall missions like the train or  escaping the clock tower but they're few  and far in between there was also an  espionage mission i wasn't expecting it  plays just like the one in black ops  cold war where you can control what's  being said and even blow your cover in  the end i'm so glad i gave this game a  shot back in 2017 and even happier to  report that i love the campaign i  accidentally skipped over and even josh  dumel i guess he's not a dick  before i move on this is a quick  reminder that black ops 4 didn't  actually have a story mode outside of  the training missions there were some  things here and there but they were  weird like mason's granddaughter and  woods being an item  that's why i love you sweetheart make no  [ __ ] sense whatsoever  anyway treyarch was reportedly very  rushed with this release and instead  focused on everything that wasn't a  campaign that gave us tons of zombies  maps multiplayer and of course blackout  i only have a few memories with blackout  but i honestly wasn't into the battle  royale scene not yet at least i think i  want to give it another chance but  that's a video for another time moving  on  [Music]  2019 was a big year for call of duty  modern warfare was back with a soft  reboot rooted in ultra realism the  campaign's tone was much darker dealing  with the war on bioweaponry and nuclear  warfare those things aren't new to call  of duty per se but if we strip away the  high octane dramatics of the original  series you get this muted realistic  story that couldn't have come at a  better time it was perfect for someone  like me who loved modern warfare because  i got to watch the series mature and  grow into this awesomely realized and  updated story  after a chemical attack on london  captain price is brought on to find and  capture the wolf a terrorist leader who  takes responsibility for the attacks  along the way he teams up with gaz  pharah alex and others to cover the  globe to put a stop to these attacks the  casting for these characters is superb  especially for captain price i love this  interview with infinity wards taylor  kurosaki and barry sloan about the  audition process and how picking barry  sloane to play captain price was a  no-brainer he was perfect for the role  as you make your way through the story  you find yourself in several intense  situations memorable missions like the  piccadilly circus and clean house force  you to act and react in some dramatic  no-win situations there are also a lot  of less is more instances for example  the mission clean house only has like 11  enemies but as a whole this game is very  memorable and replayable the strong  characters realistic settings and story  accomplished what they set out to do  bring back the modern warfare series and  live up to the legacy it set more than a  decade prior modern warfare 19 was a  massive success so much so that i don't  even think they knew what they had this  game made such an impact and while it  got the yearly cycle of support from  infinity ward and activision fans like  me were sad to see that support fade  away in order for them to move on to  other projects  now of course this happens what are you  new it's activision it's the way they've  done business for years but this felt  special fans agreed that a whole another  year of modern warfare support could  have worked but we'll never truly know  well at least there's always modern  warfare 2019 part 2.  until then i can't say enough good  things about this game it was the most  excited i've been to play a call of duty  in years and of course warzone was an  extension of this using the same game  engine weapons and maps it was another  attempt at a battle royale an attempt  that exploded  millions of players cod fans are not  swarmed to warzone and helped it become  one of the biggest free to play games  ever  even after the constant issues with  download sizes weapon balancing hacker  accounts it's still a massive success  one that supports cross-console and  cross-gen it also extended the warzone  story admittedly it was nothing  groundbreaking but hey if you offer me  more modern warfare i will take it  if modern warfare 2019 was the steaming  hot cup of coffee then black ops cold  war was the cool cup of sweet tea to  calm me down  seeing this cool 80s marketing and  trailers got me pretty pumped and that  was because at the time of this  announcement i still hadn't played black  ops 2 or 3. so seeing familiar faces  like woods mason and hudson and no sign  of the future was cool and that's what  this game is cool it takes elements from  the first game like brainwashing and  espionage and gives it a fun 80s kick  with more vibrancy and even humor  not a [ __ ] word down while we go back  to the faster paced arcade style of  gameplay it totally works for this game  you're bell a cia operative trying to  take down perseus a soviet rebel who  hates america and wants to nuke it into  the ground you don't have much to go on  as perseus works in the shadows all you  have are some shaky leads and this one  [ __ ] picture that burns into your  retinas you and your team sneak around  europe in search of clues and leads and  of course you're always two steps behind  it's not all bad though missions always  keep you on your toes and there's plenty  of misdirection intense moments and  decisions to be made  missions with woods and mason are also a  real treat there are even some callbacks  to the first black ops game like the  snow base and of course the numbers  program  working alongside adler it's fun too  he's as smooth as the other guys  smoother if you count the sunglasses  like seriously he never takes them off  not even in the espionage mission  elevator conversation always gets  awkward he's a walking man of mystery  and while he's easy to work with  trusting him is another story the truth  is he can't trust you either  it turns out your character belle has  been brainwashed by adler and the cia  essentially to help them out like a  puppet you can help adler and stop  perseus and his massive nuclear launch  or say and let america burn and kill  everyone woods mason adler everyone  playing through this was weird but it's  technically not canon it's a fun  approach to wield this much power and  shift the game's ending entirely both  endings are good and worth exploring as  none of them truly interfere with the  original black ops series they've  extended this story into multiplayer and  warzone but naturally the pacing is a  lot slower because it's seasonal based  but cold war as a standalone title is a  fun neon step back into the 80s with  familiar faces and an engaging story  especially for classic black ops fans  if you've made it to this point in the  video i can't thank you enough  call of duty has obviously been a huge  part of my life for the last 15 years or  so and there are so many creative people  to thank for these games and the  memories that go with them but i also  did want to thank you guys for watching  because this took a lot of time writing  production and i don't do this to hear  myself talk i do this to share stories  with you guys so if you have call of  duty stories of your own let me know  down in the comments like this video if  you like this video and i'll see you  next time
 

